  "missed": [
    {
      "point": "The film plays fair like a magic act. Clues such as the dead canary and the goldfish bowl carried by Ching Ling Soo are visible early, so the finale reads as confirmation of what the camera showed rather than a cheat.",
      "source": "https://strangehorizons.com/non-fiction/reviews/the-prestige-the-film-and-the-screenplay/"
    },
    {
      "point": "The deaths are mirrored. Angier drowns in a tank like his wife did, and Borden goes to the gallows as his own wife's death echoes, so the story keeps circling the same few wounds.",
      "source": "https://strangehorizons.com/non-fiction/reviews/the-prestige-the-film-and-the-screenplay/"
    },
    {
      "point": "The film is a heavy reworking of the novel. The Nolans cut the spiritualism subplot and swapped the modern-day frame for Borden awaiting the gallows, changes Priest approved of.",
